  "question": {
    "text": "If \\(I=\\int \\frac{x^2 d x}{(x \\sin x+\\cos x)^2}=f(x)+\\tan x+c\\), then \\(f(x)\\) is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "kEyAs",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{\\sin x}{x \\sin x+\\cos x}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Vifcr",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{1}{(x \\sin x+\\cos x)^2}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "4hZ7h",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{-x}{\\cos x(x \\sin x+\\cos x)}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "CeyGo",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{1}{\\sin x(x \\cos x+\\sin x)}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hint : \\(I=\\int \\frac{x^2}{(x \\sin x+\\cos x)^2} d x=\\int \\frac{x}{(x \\sin x+\\cos x)^2} \\times \\frac{x}{\\cos x} d x\\)\n\\(I=-\\frac{1}{(x \\sin x+\\cos x)} \\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cos x}+\\int \\frac{1}{(x \\sin x+\\cos x)} \\cdot \\frac{(\\cos x+x \\sin x)}{\\cos ^2 x} d x\\)\n\\(\\begin{aligned} & I=-\\frac{1}{(x \\sin x+\\cos x)} \\cdot \\frac{x}{\\cos x}+\\int \\sec ^2 x d x \\\\ & I=-\\frac{x}{(x \\sin x+\\cos x) \\cos x}+\\tan x+C \\\\ & \\therefore f(x)=\\frac{-x}{\\cos x(x \\sin x+C)}\\end{aligned}\\)",
    "image": null
  },
